{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC AUDIT before closing — and it found a REAL GAP, so this story gets a fifth change (Gerrit **1728**).\n\nI checked every test this story's ACs name against merged `main` rather than assuming the landed changes covered them. Three names did not exist:\n\n| AC names | actually landed as |\n|---|---|\n| `test_cloud_cutover_converges` | `test_cloud_cutover_sends_structured_adf` + `test_lossy_body_converges_across_passes` / `test_cutover_does_not_mass_rewrite_already_synced_bodies` |\n| `test_dc_cutover_converges` | `test_dc_cutover_sends_rendered_wiki` + the same convergence pair |\n| `test_dc_comment_diff_unchanged_by_cutover` | **nothing — genuinely absent** |\n\nThe first two are naming drift: the predecessor split each \"posts once AND converges\" AC into a send test and a convergence test, which is better decomposition, and both halves are covered. Not a gap.\n\nThe third was a real hole, and a consequential one. The cutover redefines `WikiTextCodec.normalize_outbound` to be derived from `to_wire`, so on DC it RENDERS instead of being the identity — and `outbound_comments._diff_comments` builds its LOCAL dedup key with exactly that operation. Had the cutover reached that seam, every already-mirrored comment's key would shift to its rendered form while Jira still holds plain text, so every comment would read as absent and **the differ would re-post the project's entire comment history** on the pass after an operator flipped a flag whose name says nothing about comments — unrecoverable from Jira's side.\n\nIt does NOT reach it: `_resolve_codec(None)` returns `_IdentityCodec` and no caller wires a real codec into the comment path (that is story `emersed-specific-mutt`'s job). So the AC's claim is TRUE — but it was true by construction and completely untested, which is the worst combination, because the change that would break it is a one-line wiring edit that looks obviously right.\n\n1728 pins it from both ends: the resolver directly (so a regression names the seam that moved, not just the symptom) and the resulting diff asserted identical with the flag off and on, over a mixed case (one already-mirrored comment, one genuinely new) so a key that shifted but still emitted something is caught too. Plus a guards-the-guard test proving the rendered form actually differs from the raw one — without it the others could pass vacuously.\n\nOne test-authoring note worth carrying: the flag is driven through `REBAR_RECONCILER_RICH_TEXT_CUTOVER` rather than a stubbed config object. The comment differ resolves the vendor adapter AND its transport from the same config, so a stub carrying only `rich_text_cutover` raises `AttributeError: 'R' object has no attribute 'backend'` and then `'_Cfg' object has no attribute 'jira'` — failures that read like product defects. The env override exercises the real resolution path and needs no stub at all.\n\nLanded so far: 1710, 1712, 1716, 1720. Outstanding: 1728."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786620638980276001,"uuid":"a79ca004-d220-413b-b873-ddb4862e698d"}